.TH AD_LAYER_JOIN_INSERT_IN_PLACE 1 "2026-09-13" "ad 2.0" "ad layers"
.SH NAME
ad_layer_join_insert_in_place \- move inserts after a join (prevent concatenation)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ad_layer_join_insert_in_place
< raw_ops > fixed_ops
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ad_layer_join_insert_in_place
handles the pattern:
.PP
.RS
.nf
join_lines(L) + insert(L, col 1, chars)+ + [keep(L, ...) | delete(L, ...)]*
.fi
.RE
.PP
The join pulls old content up to line L. Then inserts are typed at
col 1 \(em in front of the old content. The user sees the new text
concatenated with the old text ("@dataclass DataProcessor:").
.PP
This layer reorders to:
.PP
.RS
.nf
insert(L+1, col 1, chars)+ + [keep(L+1, ...) | delete(L+1, ...)]* + join_lines(L)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The inserts and deletes now happen on line L+1 (where the content
originally was, before the join). The join happens AFTER \(em it joins
the (empty) line L with the (modified) line L+1. No concatenation.
.PP
Only matches when there are inserts at col 1 after the join. If the
ops after the join are only keeps and deletes (no inserts), the join
is needed to bring the content to the right position \(em don't reorder.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log pattern matches and transformations to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-help, \-h
Show help and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ad_layer_join_insert_in_place < raw_ops.txt > fixed_ops.txt
Move inserts after joins.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ad_layer_line_delete_in_place (1),
.BR ad_layer_split_in_place (1),
.BR ad_postprocess (1)
